{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788998853,"nonce":"D5P7ii/bRao3Mk5MwWFhSjNWPSU=","message":"The remaining item -- the interpreter's Incr/Neg/Mul on HUI8/HUI16 wrapping at 32 bits -- is UNREACHABLE by anything we can run, so it is not worth fixing blind.\n\nScanned every .hl in crates/ash/test/tests plus the entire Haxe 4.3.6 conformance tree (unit, sys, threads, eventLoop, misc), counting opcodes whose destination register is typed HUI8 or HUI16. Zero sites. No Incr, Decr, Neg or Mul writes such a register anywhere.\n\nConfirmed the divergence is real by reading all three sides: HashLink's OIncr does fetch32 + op32(INC) then store, and store does copy(ctx, \u0026r-\u003estack, v, r-\u003esize) -- 1 byte for a UI8 register, so it wraps at the byte. ash's LLVM loads at reg_types[dst], i8 for HUI8, so it wraps at the byte too. ash's interpreter does as_i32().wrapping_add(1) into a NaN-boxed i32, which has no declared width to truncate to, so it keeps all 32 bits. The interpreter is the odd one out.\n\nWhy it should not be fixed yet: the oracle is HashLink, stock hl does not run on Apple Silicon, and there is no program that exercises the path -- so every test stays green whether the masking is right or wrong, on an opcode as hot as the interpreter gets. Untestable and hot is the worst combination to change.\n\nSequence when it is taken up: build a reproducer first (a Haxe program putting arithmetic on a UI8-typed local, probably via an hl.UI8 extern, or a hand-built .hl), confirm the three-way disagreement on the NUC where hl runs, then mask in the interpreter. The null-guard work in bfe4155 and 318bd3c contributes nothing here -- different side, different oracle, and interp-vs-jit agreement is the one check that would pass while still being wrong.","files":null}]}